WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.860
You know, when you pop the hood of a car, because something is making a weird noise,

00:07.520 --> 00:11.600
there is this very specific expectation of what you are going to find in there.

00:11.640 --> 00:13.220
Right. You expect to see physical parts.

00:13.520 --> 00:20.240
Exactly. Yeah. You expect to see gears, belts, metal components physically interacting with one another.

00:20.360 --> 00:21.860
I mean, it's mechanical. It is visible.

00:22.100 --> 00:26.040
Yeah. You look at a spinning rubber belt and you can point your finger right at it

00:26.040 --> 00:29.380
and definitively say, you know, that belt is turning that pulley.

00:30.000 --> 00:31.780
Which is turning that alternator.

00:32.160 --> 00:37.280
Right. And the human mind naturally craves that kind of tangible physical causality.

00:37.400 --> 00:42.660
We are tool builders. So we want to see object A strike object B to produce outcome C.

00:43.020 --> 00:46.740
Absolutely. When we can trace the kinetic energy through a system like that,

00:46.820 --> 00:48.460
well, we feel like we truly understand it.

00:48.600 --> 00:52.740
But imagine if you pop the hood of your car and instead of belts and gears, you just saw empty space.

00:53.100 --> 00:59.240
And the engine was turning entirely because of like a complex symphony of sound waves echoing in the engine bay.

00:59.240 --> 01:00.240
That would be terrifying.

01:00.640 --> 01:08.340
It would. Or to push it into an even weirder territory, what if the car wasn't being driven by physical parts or waves at all,

01:08.660 --> 01:14.280
but by this invisible mathematical map of every possible road you could take in the future?

01:14.480 --> 01:17.700
Which is a completely wild concept to try and wrap your head around.

01:17.800 --> 01:23.640
It really is. And as you are listening to my voice right now, whether you are sitting in your car or walking your dog,

01:23.640 --> 01:27.020
I want you to think about the engine inside your own skull.

01:27.500 --> 01:27.800
The brain?

01:28.000 --> 01:34.420
Yeah, the brain. How is your brain actually coordinating the thought you are having at this exact millisecond?

01:34.540 --> 01:41.540
Like, how are you able to hold my words in your mind, process their meaning, and seamlessly adapt to the very next sentence I say?

01:41.940 --> 01:44.740
We are talking about the ultimate mechanical mystery here.

01:44.840 --> 01:51.000
I mean, for over a century, neuroscience has been desperately trying to find the gears and the belts inside the cortex.

01:51.000 --> 01:53.460
Because we want that tangible A to B connection.

01:53.560 --> 02:00.480
Exactly. We want to point to a specific biological structure and say, there, that is the physical thing that causes a thought.

02:00.840 --> 02:10.420
And we have a single absolutely fascinating source for today's deep dive that takes a sledgehammer to that entire physical gear paradigm.

02:10.600 --> 02:12.340
It really does. It's a fantastic paper.

02:12.340 --> 02:20.580
Yeah, it's an independent research paper published in July 2026 by an author named Flyxion, and the title itself is just a massive provocation.

02:20.900 --> 02:29.060
From wave dynamics to continuation dynamics, why neural oscillations may be realizations rather than explanations.

02:29.460 --> 02:38.200
Just a great title. And the stakes here are massive, you know, because there is this fierce debate happening right now in neuroscience about what actually controls your mind.

02:38.200 --> 02:50.320
Right. On one side, you have this very prominent, established theory arguing that brain waves, these literal, measurable electrical oscillations rippling across your cortex, are the actual machinery of cognitive control.

02:50.400 --> 02:51.560
They are the belts and pulleys.

02:51.640 --> 02:56.460
Yeah. This established view is widely known as spateful computing, or wave-based control.

02:56.720 --> 03:02.120
Okay, so prominent researchers argue that these rhythmic electrical waves are actively routing information in the brain.

03:02.120 --> 03:14.460
Exactly. In their view, the waves are dynamically opening and closing communication pathways between different regions of your brain, and they say that is what allows you to think flexibly and adapt to new situations.

03:14.720 --> 03:18.520
But then Flyxion steps into the ring and suggests something deeply unsettling.

03:19.220 --> 03:24.760
Flyxion proposes that these brain waves might just be, well, biological exhaust fumes.

03:24.760 --> 03:26.800
That's a brutal way to put it, but yeah.

03:27.240 --> 03:34.440
Or, to be a bit more charitable, they are merely a biological side effect of a much deeper, completely hidden mathematical architecture.

03:34.640 --> 03:38.120
And that hidden architecture is the true conductor of your mind.

03:38.500 --> 03:41.180
An architecture they call admissible continuations.

03:41.440 --> 03:41.720
Right.

03:42.040 --> 03:45.680
Flyxion is performing this surgical deconstruction of the established science.

03:45.680 --> 03:55.060
I mean, they are looking at the exact same data, the exact same biological observations from the wave theorists, and pointing out a fundamental logical fallacy.

03:55.240 --> 03:56.480
Which is what, exactly?

03:56.720 --> 04:03.760
That correlating a physical process, like a brain wave, with the act of thinking, does not prove that the wave actually causes the thinking.

04:03.840 --> 04:07.660
Oh, wow. Okay, so correlation is not causation, even at the level of brain waves.

04:07.660 --> 04:08.400
Yeah, exactly.

04:08.900 --> 04:21.600
Flyxion is forcing the scientific community to separate what is strictly mathematically necessary for cognition from what just happens to be the biological hardware our species evolved to use.

04:21.600 --> 04:28.000
And the reason you, the listener, need to care about this is that this isn't just, you know, academic bickering over esoteric math.

04:28.080 --> 04:31.240
This is about the fundamental physics of your own identity.

04:31.640 --> 04:37.440
Because we are trying to understand the exact literal mechanism that allows you to learn a new skill.

04:37.660 --> 04:37.880
Right.

04:38.140 --> 04:40.840
Or to change your mind or recover from a mistake.

04:41.000 --> 04:41.160
Yeah.

04:41.200 --> 04:45.900
Or even just to maintain a continuous sense of you over the course of an entire lifetime.

04:46.080 --> 04:49.940
I mean, if we don't actually know what is driving the car, we don't really know who we are.

04:49.940 --> 05:00.020
And science often takes its biggest leaps, not by discovering brand new data, but by aggressively questioning the assumptions we've just, you know, draped over the data we already possess.

05:00.360 --> 05:04.140
We have to ask ourselves if we are mistaking the instrument for the music.

05:04.140 --> 05:18.080
Like, so to even begin to grasp why Flyxion's mathematical map is so revolutionary, we have to start with the universally agreed upon problem that the brain is desperately trying to solve every single second you are awake.

05:18.220 --> 05:18.420
Right.

05:18.500 --> 05:19.300
The rowing problem.

05:19.500 --> 05:19.720
Yeah.

05:19.880 --> 05:26.500
But before we get into the modern view, I want to paint a picture of how we used to think the brain worked, just to show how far we've actually come.

05:26.720 --> 05:28.080
The old connectionist view.

05:28.080 --> 05:38.180
Exactly. For a long time, the dominant model was this connectionist view. And I think of it as a strict, bureaucratic hierarchy of highly specialized workers.

05:38.460 --> 05:44.280
Yeah, where every single neuron in your brain had one very specific permanent job.

05:44.360 --> 05:48.700
Right. Which gave rise to that famous hypothetical concept, the grandmother cell.

05:48.820 --> 05:52.780
Oh, the grandmother cell is a perfect encapsulation of that whole era of neuroscience.

05:52.780 --> 06:04.580
The idea was that deep in your cortex, there was a single, highly specialized neuron. And its entire purpose in life, its only function, was to fire an electrical spike when you saw your grandmother's face.

06:04.740 --> 06:05.980
Just that one face.

06:06.380 --> 06:15.320
Right. And conversely, if you saw a coffee cup, a completely different dedicated neuron would fire. If you heard a dog bark, yet another specific neuron would activate.

06:15.320 --> 06:27.560
It sounds so clean and organized, you know, like this perfect assembly line of feature detectors. But if one cell does one thing, how on earth do I learn new things without completely running out of cells?

06:27.780 --> 06:39.340
Well, that is precisely the fatal flaw of the grandmother cell theory. The human cortex has roughly 16 billion neurons. Which, you know, sounds like a lot.

06:39.340 --> 06:40.420
It does sound like a lot.

06:40.540 --> 06:50.160
But if every single concept, memory, visual input, and motor command required its own dedicated hardware, your brain would reach full storage capacity incredibly quickly.

06:50.280 --> 06:52.220
You'd run out of room by the time you were in kindergarten.

06:52.340 --> 06:57.200
Pretty much, yeah. The inefficiency of a strictly specialized system is biologically impossible.

06:57.960 --> 07:05.480
So when modern neuroscientists actually started recording from live, behaving brains with high-density electrodes, they found something entirely different.

07:05.700 --> 07:07.520
They discovered mixed selectivity.

07:07.520 --> 07:09.940
Exactly. Mixed selectivity.

07:10.240 --> 07:23.180
Let me try to visualize this for the listener. So the old assembly line view is like a massive factory where every worker has a specific wrench and they only tighten one specific bolt on one specific car door forever.

07:23.340 --> 07:23.460
Right.

07:23.780 --> 07:28.420
But the real brain operating on mixed selectivity is more like a repertory theater company.

07:28.560 --> 07:29.400
Oh, I like that analogy.

07:29.400 --> 07:40.000
Yeah, so you have the exact same troupe of 50 actors, but on Tuesday night, they are putting on a heavy tragedy, and your lead actor is playing the prince.

07:40.160 --> 07:40.520
Okay.

07:40.800 --> 07:48.760
On Wednesday night, that exact same troupe is putting on a slapstick comedy, and that very same lead actor is now playing a bumbling sidekick.

07:48.760 --> 07:53.840
Yeah, the actors or the neurons can do almost anything depending on the script they are handed that night.

07:53.900 --> 07:54.180
Right.

07:54.340 --> 08:12.180
That captures the biological reality beautifully. A neuron in your prefrontal cortex might participate in holding a visual memory right now, but a fraction of a second later, if the context of your environment changes, that exact same neuron might be used to help route a motor command to your arm.

08:12.180 --> 08:13.480
So they are multitasking.

08:13.840 --> 08:25.180
Heavily. A given task only engages a specific fraction of the available neurons, and those neurons multiplex. They share the workload across millions of different overlapping combinations.

08:26.020 --> 08:30.500
Which creates an almost infinite combinatorial explosion of flexibility.

08:30.740 --> 08:36.280
Exactly. That is what allows you to learn and adapt without your brain ever filling up, so to speak.

08:36.280 --> 08:46.480
But wait, I'm getting stuck here. If I have a theater company of 50 actors, and they are constantly changing roles night to night, minute to minute, who is directing the play?

08:46.600 --> 08:48.080
That is the million-dollar question.

08:48.300 --> 08:56.960
Like, who is handing out the scripts? How do the actors know that tonight is the tragedy and not the comedy, without the whole production instantly falling into total chaos?

08:57.300 --> 09:03.020
You've just articulated the control problem. This is the massive central hurdle in modern neuroscience.

09:03.020 --> 09:12.440
The brain system must rapidly, on the sub-second timescale, figure out which neurons are supposed to contribute to which computation from moment to moment.

09:12.460 --> 09:14.400
It has to route the information dynamically.

09:14.880 --> 09:18.160
Yes. And the terrifying part is that there is no director.

09:18.700 --> 09:26.220
The brain doesn't have a tiny, homunculus, you know, a little man sitting in a control room behind your eyes, flipping switches and handing out scripts.

09:26.220 --> 09:33.140
So the biological network has to completely organize itself continuously without a central boss.

09:33.220 --> 09:35.920
Exactly. Which brings us to the wave theorists.

09:36.780 --> 09:43.740
Researchers like Miller, Bryn Katt, and Roy look at this terrifying boss-less theater company, and they propose a solution.

09:44.340 --> 09:46.960
And their solution relies on brain waves.

09:47.080 --> 09:54.140
Right. They look at the physical wires connecting the neurons, the synapses, and they say there is no physical way the wiring alone can act as the director.

09:54.140 --> 09:55.840
Because it's just static wire.

09:56.080 --> 09:59.740
Yeah. They base this on a claim of sheer biological necessity.

10:00.280 --> 10:11.980
The spatial computing argument is that physically rewiring synapses, or even just tracking the individual synaptic assignments for millions of neurons on a millisecond-by-millisecond basis, is far too slow and cumbersome.

10:12.040 --> 10:14.540
It's just physically impossible to rewire that fast.

10:14.780 --> 10:20.200
Exactly. To use their exact phrasing, it is an implausible computational demand.

10:20.200 --> 10:28.820
They argue that a static web of physical connections simply cannot route information dynamically enough to explain human thought.

10:29.080 --> 10:34.180
So they need a director. They need something external to the wiring to manage the chaos.

10:34.520 --> 10:40.700
Yes. They argue there must be a low-dimensional control signal layered over the top of the physical wiring.

10:40.920 --> 10:45.220
And they identify spatially patterned brain waves as that signal.

10:45.220 --> 10:49.460
Okay. So imagine an electrical wave sweeping across the surface of the cortex.

10:49.720 --> 10:57.480
Right. The wave has peaks and troughs. Miller's theory suggests that the specific phase and frequency of these waves act as a gating mechanism.

10:57.640 --> 10:59.840
A gating mechanism. Meaning what, exactly?

11:00.100 --> 11:05.280
Meaning when a neuron is caught in the peak of the wave, it is allowed to express its information.

11:06.080 --> 11:08.760
But when it is caught in the trough, it is silenced.

11:08.880 --> 11:13.160
Oh, I see. So to bring it back to our theater company, the brain waves are the stage lighting.

11:13.160 --> 11:13.780
Yes. Perfect.

11:14.080 --> 11:19.280
The wiring of the brain is fixed. The actors are all standing on stage in the dark and they don't know what play they're in.

11:19.520 --> 11:21.700
But the brain wave comes in like a spotlight.

11:22.180 --> 11:25.740
And whoever is illuminated by the spotlight speaks their lines.

11:26.380 --> 11:28.240
Whoever is in the dark stays quiet.

11:28.520 --> 11:32.060
Wow. So by rapidly sweeping that spotlight around the stage,

11:32.220 --> 11:38.060
you can instantly change the entire narrative of the play without having to physically move the actors or rewrite the script.

11:38.060 --> 11:45.980
And because this mechanism elegantly solves the control problem, Miller's team concludes that waves are computationally necessary.

11:46.240 --> 11:53.820
So in their framework, without the waves, the brain is just a dumb static net of wires incapable of flexible thought.

11:53.980 --> 11:57.180
Exactly. The wave is the fundamental requirement for cognition.

11:57.180 --> 11:59.880
But I still don't buy that it just happens, you know?

12:00.120 --> 12:09.860
Like if the physical wiring is just a static web, how is it physically possible for a signal to take a different route without something like a wave acting as a switch man on the train tracks?

12:09.980 --> 12:13.120
Right. It feels like there has to be an active force shifting the tracks.

12:13.260 --> 12:13.560
Doesn't.

12:13.700 --> 12:18.380
Well, that intuition is exactly why the wave theory is so popular. It just feels necessary.

12:18.980 --> 12:23.460
But Flyxion points out that this feeling of necessity is scientifically hollow.

12:23.780 --> 12:24.300
Hollow how?

12:24.300 --> 12:26.920
Because Flyxion doesn't just argue this philosophically.

12:27.320 --> 12:36.680
They point to concrete, undeniable proof that static wiring can, and actually does, solve this exact routing problem without any waves at all.

12:36.760 --> 12:38.660
Okay, now I'm hooked. What is the proof?

12:39.100 --> 12:45.200
We have to look at a landmark 2013 study by Monte, Sauccio, Chenoy, and Newsom.

12:45.720 --> 12:52.920
Oh, I was reading the Monte study in the source material and it completely broke my brain because it seems to prove the exact opposite of what Miller is claiming.

12:53.040 --> 12:53.700
It really does.

12:53.700 --> 12:55.660
They built a digital brain without waves for us.

12:55.660 --> 12:58.780
Let's break down exactly what Monte and his team did because it's brilliant.

12:59.220 --> 13:00.200
They started with biology.

13:00.740 --> 13:08.400
They recorded the prefrontal cortex activity in macaque monkeys that were performing a highly complex, context-dependent task.

13:08.500 --> 13:11.740
Okay, so a live monkey doing a task. What was the task?

13:12.100 --> 13:16.060
The monkey sits in front of a screen displaying a cloud of moving dots.

13:16.400 --> 13:21.120
Some dots are moving left, some are moving right, and they're different colors, say red and green.

13:21.120 --> 13:26.200
So a messy, complex visual input, lots of competing information.

13:26.580 --> 13:30.720
Very messy. Now the researchers would give the monkey a cue before the dots appeared.

13:31.020 --> 13:32.140
Like a light or a sound.

13:32.460 --> 13:39.080
Yeah, exactly. And sometimes the cue meant, tell me which direction the majority of the dots are moving, and completely ignore the color.

13:39.240 --> 13:39.460
Okay.

13:39.460 --> 13:44.500
But other times the cue meant, tell me the dominant color, and completely ignore the motion.

13:44.920 --> 13:45.680
Oh, wow.

13:46.420 --> 13:48.420
This is mixed selectivity in action.

13:48.940 --> 13:52.920
The exact same visual input hits the monkey's retinas every single time,

13:53.420 --> 13:59.540
but the brain has to route that information completely differently based on an invisible internal rule.

13:59.540 --> 14:02.920
Exactly. And the monkeys perform the task beautifully.

14:03.660 --> 14:07.960
The researchers recorded exactly how the neural populations manage this complex routing.

14:08.160 --> 14:09.500
But that was just phase one.

14:09.680 --> 14:12.860
Right. The true genius of the Monted paper was the second phase.

14:13.320 --> 14:17.260
The researchers built an artificial recurrent neural network, an RNN.

14:17.600 --> 14:20.100
So this is a computer model simulated on a server.

14:20.340 --> 14:23.540
Yes. It consists of artificial neurons connected by artificial synopsis.

14:23.540 --> 14:30.580
And crucially, it is recurrent, meaning the connections loop back on themselves, creating these complex feedback loops.

14:30.960 --> 14:33.600
But what is missing from this digital brain?

14:34.020 --> 14:40.120
Brain waves, spatially patterned oscillations, gating mechanisms, spotlights, all of it.

14:40.320 --> 14:41.100
None of it is there.

14:41.260 --> 14:45.640
None of it. The RNN has absolutely no external control signal.

14:46.040 --> 14:52.660
It is nothing but a static web of connections, passing numbers back and forth through fixed mathematical weights.

14:52.660 --> 14:54.760
Okay. And then they trained it.

14:54.900 --> 15:02.480
Yes. They trained this waveless digital network to perform the exact same context-dependent dot task the monkeys were doing.

15:02.740 --> 15:05.720
But wait. If Miller is right, this should be impossible.

15:06.580 --> 15:11.500
A static web of wires is supposed to face an implausible computational demand.

15:11.680 --> 15:15.780
It shouldn't be able to route the information without a wave to act as the traffic cop.

15:15.920 --> 15:17.220
Yet the RNN solved it.

15:17.560 --> 15:18.040
Flawlessly.

15:18.040 --> 15:27.540
Through the process of training, the fixed weights of the artificial synapses arranged themselves so that the internal recurrent dynamics naturally separated the information.

15:27.900 --> 15:29.480
How does that even work without a director?

15:29.960 --> 15:33.640
The network created invisible mathematical valleys, attractor states.

15:34.300 --> 15:41.540
When the color context was active, the network's internal feedback loops naturally pulled the activity down one valley, ignoring the motion data.

15:41.820 --> 15:43.480
And when the motion context was active?

15:43.640 --> 15:45.480
The activities flowed down a different valley.

15:45.480 --> 15:48.060
So the actors just figured it out among themselves.

15:48.520 --> 15:49.360
Essentially, yes.

15:49.440 --> 16:03.840
The connections between them were so finely tuned that the moment actor A set a line in a tragic tone, the feedback loop instantly informed all the other actors to switch to the tragedy script, and there was no director or spotlight required.

16:04.200 --> 16:05.620
That is the perfect way to put it.

16:05.900 --> 16:13.420
The recurrent loops hold the context in memory, and the geometry of the network weights acts as the routing mechanism itself.

16:13.720 --> 16:14.880
That is wild.

16:14.880 --> 16:15.540
It is.

16:16.020 --> 16:18.740
But Flicksheet is incredibly careful with this finding.

16:19.420 --> 16:22.040
I mean, they aren't saying that human brain waves don't exist.

16:22.140 --> 16:24.760
We could put an EEG on your scalp right now and measure them.

16:24.780 --> 16:26.100
Right, they are definitely there.

16:26.280 --> 16:27.780
They aren't even saying waves are useless.

16:27.960 --> 16:30.800
Maybe they make the biological brain more energetically efficient.

16:31.300 --> 16:35.060
But the RNN completely incinerates the necessity claim.

16:35.060 --> 16:47.620
Because if a computer model with zero waves can solve the exact same context-dependent routing problem, then waves cannot be the unique foundational prerequisite for flexible thought.

16:47.620 --> 16:48.180
Exactly.

16:48.480 --> 16:49.780
They aren't strictly necessary.

16:49.780 --> 16:54.860
But surely Miller's team at MIT knows about recurrent neural networks, right?

16:55.400 --> 17:00.020
They know about how population geometry and subspace coding can organize information.

17:00.440 --> 17:03.400
How do they justify the wave theory in the face of all that?

17:03.400 --> 17:09.060
The irony in the scientific literature is staggering here, and Flicksheet explicitly calls it out.

17:09.060 --> 17:15.060
In Miller's paper, Defending Spatial Computing, they actually cite the population dynamics literature.

17:15.140 --> 17:15.680
You're kidding.

17:15.680 --> 17:20.880
No, they cite researchers like Kauffman and Vias who study subspace coding.

17:21.480 --> 17:27.960
That is the exact phenomenon where neural activity organizes into these low-dimensional geometric patterns to avoid interference.

17:28.200 --> 17:28.400
Okay.

17:28.980 --> 17:35.300
Miller cites this as evidence that the brain needs low-dimensional control and then claims waves are that control.

17:35.420 --> 17:37.740
Wait, they're citing the literature that disproves them?

17:37.940 --> 17:38.340
Yes.

17:38.720 --> 17:43.860
They're citing the structural outcome while ignoring the mechanism that the literature actually provides.

17:43.960 --> 17:44.320
Wow.

17:44.320 --> 17:52.540
Kauffman, B.S., and Monte all demonstrate that this beautiful subspace geometry emerges purely from fixed recurrent connectivity.

17:52.940 --> 17:55.600
You do not need a wave field to generate a subspace.

17:55.600 --> 17:59.980
So Miller absorbs the evidence of how the brain organizes information,

18:00.280 --> 18:08.640
but seemingly misses that the mechanism generating that organization in those very papers is static wiring, not oscillations.

18:08.820 --> 18:10.740
Exactly. It's a huge blind spot.

18:10.740 --> 18:16.240
So if Miller's wave theory isn't the fundamental answer, the explanatory burden shifts completely.

18:16.900 --> 18:19.660
If we take away the waves, what actually is the foundation?

18:20.220 --> 18:26.040
Like, what do we know for an absolute fact stays stable when a biological brain learns a new skill?

18:26.300 --> 18:30.960
To find the real bedrock of learning, we have to look at brain-computer interfaces, or BCIs.

18:30.960 --> 18:33.420
Hooking the brain up to a computer.

18:33.700 --> 18:33.980
Yes.

18:34.440 --> 18:40.820
The most revealing experiments about the physical constraints of the brain come from physically hooking the cortex up to a machine.

18:41.300 --> 18:49.140
Sadler and Gollum ran a series of BCI experiments with monkeys in 2014 and 2018 that completely redefined our understanding of the brain's physical limits.

18:49.340 --> 18:52.400
Walk us through the physical setup of these BCI experiments.

18:52.400 --> 18:54.700
What is actually happening inside the monkey's skull?

18:54.700 --> 19:02.340
So a surgeon implants a tiny square grid called a Utah array directly into the primary motor cortex of a macaque monkey.

19:02.440 --> 19:03.400
And how big is this array?

19:03.640 --> 19:04.540
It's microscopic.

19:05.400 --> 19:11.360
This array has a hundred tiny metal needles, and each one rests near a single neuron.

19:11.880 --> 19:17.280
The array records the electrical firing rates of those 100 specific neurons in real time.

19:17.280 --> 19:23.240
And then a computer algorithm translates those 100 firing rates into the movement of a cursor on a screen.

19:23.560 --> 19:23.940
Exactly.

19:24.440 --> 19:29.440
The monkey is trained to move the cursor to targets just by thinking about moving its arm.

19:30.220 --> 19:38.760
Now, theoretically, if you have a hundred neurons, and each one can fire fast, slow, or not at all, the number of possible combinations is astronomical.

19:38.980 --> 19:41.680
That's a massive, multidimensional space of possibility.

19:41.800 --> 19:42.420
Theoretically, it is.

19:42.420 --> 19:43.760
But biologically, no.

19:44.140 --> 19:47.780
Sadler and Golub found that those 100 neurons do not fire independently.

19:48.460 --> 19:55.500
Because they are physically wired together by a dense, localized web of biological synapses, their activity is highly constrained.

19:55.600 --> 19:56.340
Constraint how?

19:56.540 --> 20:01.160
If neuron 1 fires, neuron 12 is highly likely to fire, and neuron 40 is suppressed.

20:01.740 --> 20:04.480
They fire in coordinated, low-dimensional patterns.

20:04.580 --> 20:07.120
They have a physical repertoire, a biological habit.

20:07.120 --> 20:07.840
Exactly.

20:08.680 --> 20:18.720
The researchers mapped all the naturally occurring patterns and found that the neural activity lives within a constrained mathematical space called the intrinsic manifold.

20:19.060 --> 20:20.320
The intrinsic manifold.

20:20.660 --> 20:20.920
Right.

20:21.360 --> 20:30.220
You can think of the manifold as the rigid envelope of what that specific network of neurons naturally knows how to do based on its physical anatomy.

20:30.480 --> 20:30.700
Okay.

20:30.800 --> 20:34.380
Let's think of those 100 neurons like a grand piano.

20:34.540 --> 20:34.900
A piano.

20:35.040 --> 20:35.180
Okay.

20:35.180 --> 20:44.180
Theoretically, you can mash your forearms on the keyboard and play a completely random, chaotic arrangement of all 88 keys at once.

20:44.260 --> 20:44.860
Sure, you're good.

20:45.160 --> 20:45.880
But you don't.

20:46.100 --> 20:54.560
Because of the physical structure of your human hands, the way your tendons stretch, and the rules of music you have practiced, you tend to play certain coordinated chords.

20:54.700 --> 20:54.900
Right.

20:55.060 --> 20:56.860
You play C major, you play G minor.

20:56.940 --> 20:57.300
Exactly.

20:57.500 --> 21:02.660
The collection of all the chords your hands physically know how to play easily, that is your intrinsic manifold.

21:02.660 --> 21:05.460
The piano analogy is highly accurate here.

21:06.180 --> 21:12.460
Now, once the monkey was proficient at playing its natural chords to move the cursor, the researchers pulled the rug out.

21:12.460 --> 21:13.460
What did they do?

21:13.460 --> 21:17.180
They secretly altered the translation algorithm on the computer.

21:17.920 --> 21:19.500
They perturbed the mapping.

21:20.000 --> 21:26.840
They effectively told the computer the neural cord that used to move the cursor UP now moves it left T.

21:26.980 --> 21:27.860
Oh, wow.

21:28.140 --> 21:31.960
So the monkey has to learn a completely new rule on the fly.

21:32.180 --> 21:32.460
Yes.

21:32.460 --> 21:35.380
And the researchers did this in two distinct ways.

21:35.520 --> 21:35.680
Right.

21:36.120 --> 21:38.580
The first type of perturbation was within manifold.

21:39.380 --> 21:46.740
The researchers changed the mapping, but the new neural pattern required to move the cursor was still one of the chords the monkey already knew how to play.

21:46.880 --> 21:49.920
So it was within the monkey's natural physical repertoire.

21:50.220 --> 21:50.520
Exactly.

21:50.520 --> 22:02.120
So using the piano again, it's like telling the pianist, okay, the new song requires you to play a standard C major chord, but the synthesizer is now going to output a drum sound instead of a piano sound.

22:02.240 --> 22:02.640
Exactly.

22:03.120 --> 22:09.380
The output changed, but the physical hand shape required is already deeply ingrained in their muscle memory.

22:09.500 --> 22:11.460
And the behavioral result was striking, right?

22:11.620 --> 22:12.580
It really was.

22:12.580 --> 22:21.840
The monkeys learned that within manifold perturbations incredibly quickly, within minutes or a few hours, their brains adapted to the new rule.

22:22.020 --> 22:26.440
They reorganized their existing cords and they were hitting the targets perfectly again.

22:26.440 --> 22:29.340
But the second type of perturbation was the deal breaker.

22:29.680 --> 22:31.520
The outside manifold perturbation.

22:31.760 --> 22:31.860
Right.

22:31.860 --> 22:43.160
The researchers altered the algorithm so that to move the cursor to the target, the monkey had to generate a pattern of neural activity that fell completely outside of its natural repertoire.

22:43.380 --> 22:50.440
It required a combination of neuronal firing that defied the physical synaptic wiring of that specific patch of cortex.

22:50.760 --> 22:51.000
Yes.

22:51.440 --> 22:56.020
So to play this new song, you need to press a key that physically does not exist on your keyboard.

22:56.340 --> 22:59.780
You need to play a note that is perfectly between E and F.

22:59.880 --> 23:01.460
And the monkeys couldn't do it.

23:01.860 --> 23:11.440
They became frustrated, their neural firing became erratic, and over the short time scale of the experiment, which was a single day, they completely failed to learn the new mapping.

23:11.620 --> 23:12.520
They just couldn't do it.

23:12.600 --> 23:12.820
No.

23:12.940 --> 23:19.280
They couldn't just magically invent a brand new, physically unsupported firing pattern out of thin air.

23:19.580 --> 23:22.800
This feels like a massive victory for the physical hardware theory.

23:23.360 --> 23:31.760
I mean, if short-term learning is dictated so strictly by the physical wiring of the synapses, it pushes the wave theory even further into irrelevance.

23:31.760 --> 23:32.560
It definitely does.

23:32.720 --> 23:35.300
You don't need a sweeping electrical spotlight to explain this.

23:35.440 --> 23:41.340
The physical covariance of the synapses, the manifold, explains the boundary of learning perfectly.

23:41.340 --> 23:44.540
Manifold theory is incredibly predictive.

23:45.260 --> 23:51.960
It quantifies exactly when learning will succeed and when it will fail based entirely on the rigid physical architecture of the network.

23:52.100 --> 23:56.700
It presents the brain as a highly structured, physically grounded machine.

23:56.700 --> 24:02.560
But if manifold theory explains the bedrock of learning so perfectly, why did Flyxion write this paper?

24:02.700 --> 24:03.820
That is the big twist.

24:04.000 --> 24:04.320
Right.

24:04.420 --> 24:11.780
Because if the physical wiring dictates the repertoire, and you can only learn what your repertoire allows, the case seems closed.

24:12.180 --> 24:13.480
The manifold is the boss.

24:13.480 --> 24:17.540
The case is closed only if you look at the brain over a period of hours.

24:17.940 --> 24:20.460
But biology is not static.

24:20.840 --> 24:21.100
Yep.

24:21.300 --> 24:27.820
If you record from a brain over days, weeks, or months, that beautiful, rigid manifold theory completely shatters.

24:28.000 --> 24:28.800
Completely shatters.

24:29.120 --> 24:29.440
Yes.

24:30.180 --> 24:34.020
This brings us to the profound mystery of representational drift.

24:34.320 --> 24:39.800
Okay, this is where the story goes from elegant to deeply, almost existentially terrifying.

24:39.800 --> 24:45.200
Walk us through Driscoll's 2017 virtual reality mice.

24:45.820 --> 24:48.380
Driscoll's team performed a technological marvel.

24:49.280 --> 24:55.000
They had mice running on spherical treadmills, surrounded by screens displaying a virtual reality maze.

24:55.080 --> 24:56.420
A VR maze for mice.

24:56.560 --> 24:56.760
Yeah.

24:57.020 --> 24:59.360
And the task was simple and repetitive.

24:59.940 --> 25:04.740
Run down the virtual corridor, see a specific cue, turn left, and get a reward.

25:04.840 --> 25:05.480
Pretty standard.

25:05.480 --> 25:17.920
But unlike the BCI monkeys, Driscoll's team managed to record from the exact same specific individual neurons in the mouse's posterior parietal cortex, the PPC, chronically, every single day for over a month.

25:18.040 --> 25:18.300
Wow.

25:18.560 --> 25:19.160
Over a month.

25:19.280 --> 25:22.720
And based on manifold theory, we have a very clear prediction for what should happen here, Aaron.

25:22.740 --> 25:23.000
You do.

25:23.340 --> 25:26.480
Once the mouse learns the maze on day one, the cord should be locked in.

25:26.480 --> 25:35.560
The synapses have established the physical repertoire required to run the maze, and as long as the mouse remembers how to do it, that physical neural pattern should look identical on day 30.

25:35.960 --> 25:37.220
That is the logical assumption.

25:38.040 --> 25:40.360
But the data showed the exact opposite.

25:40.520 --> 25:41.120
What happened?

25:41.220 --> 25:46.940
Over days and weeks, the activity-behavior relationships of the individual neurons completely reconfigured.

25:46.940 --> 25:56.540
A specific neuron that fired vigorously when the mouse turned left on day one might, by day 14, be completely silent during the turn.

25:56.700 --> 25:57.100
Silent?

25:57.220 --> 25:58.140
Then what is it doing?

25:58.600 --> 26:00.740
It might only fire when the mouse was running straight.

26:01.320 --> 26:05.400
And by day 30, that same neuron might only fire when the mouse was drinking water.

26:05.620 --> 26:09.240
The cells are just abandoning their posts and taking on completely new jobs.

26:09.300 --> 26:09.500
Yeah.

26:09.640 --> 26:10.900
It was a near-total scramble.

26:10.900 --> 26:20.880
The manifold itself, the physical covariance structure of the population, the very thing that Sadler and Gullab proved was the rigid, unyielding anchor of learning.

26:21.300 --> 26:23.500
That anchor simply dissolved and drifted away.

26:23.600 --> 26:24.520
It drifted away.

26:24.660 --> 26:24.820
Yeah.

26:25.360 --> 26:30.220
The physical hardware map of the maze morphed into something completely unrecognizable from week one.

26:30.340 --> 26:30.980
But wait.

26:31.320 --> 26:38.420
If the physical hardware map that stores the memory of the maze completely scrambles itself over a month, the mouse must forget the maze.

26:38.420 --> 26:41.460
It should be bumping into the virtual walls like it has amnesia.

26:41.740 --> 26:45.620
That is the twist that keeps neuroscientists awake at night.

26:45.840 --> 26:46.160
What?

26:46.700 --> 26:50.320
The mice's behavior and task performance did not degrade at all.

26:50.480 --> 26:51.460
You're kidding.

26:51.520 --> 26:51.860
Nope.

26:52.360 --> 26:57.680
They ran the maze flawlessly on day one, and they ran it flawlessly on day 30.

26:58.160 --> 26:59.980
The behavior was perfectly stable.

27:00.460 --> 27:04.940
But the physical neural representation driving that behavior was totally unstable.

27:04.940 --> 27:09.960
I really want you, the listener, to internalize how bizarre this is.

27:10.360 --> 27:13.520
I mean, we tend to use analogies like the ship of Theseus for this.

27:14.020 --> 27:18.040
You know, if you replace every plank on a wooden ship, is it still the same ship?

27:18.160 --> 27:18.460
Right.

27:18.620 --> 27:20.180
The classic philosophy problem.

27:20.220 --> 27:21.800
But that feels too philosophical.

27:22.240 --> 27:22.920
Let's make it modern.

27:23.280 --> 27:24.980
Imagine you have a banking app on your phone.

27:25.080 --> 27:25.560
You open it.

27:25.600 --> 27:26.160
You see your balance.

27:26.240 --> 27:26.900
You transfer money.

27:26.960 --> 27:27.800
It works perfectly.

27:27.800 --> 27:35.600
But unbeknownst to you, every single night, a team of chaotic engineers completely rewrites

27:35.600 --> 27:40.460
the back-end database code from scratch, using different programming languages, different

27:40.460 --> 27:43.060
server architectures, and different routing logic.

27:43.200 --> 27:45.840
The underlying code is a completely different beast every morning.

27:46.220 --> 27:46.540
Yes.

27:46.940 --> 27:51.540
But when you open the app, the buttons are in the exact same place, and the money transfers

27:51.540 --> 27:52.280
flawlessly.

27:52.940 --> 27:56.880
How is the app surviving the complete destruction and rebuilding of its own code?

27:56.880 --> 27:58.420
It shouldn't be able to.

27:59.160 --> 28:03.920
The field of neuroscience panicked a bit at the Driscoll data, honestly, and they attempted

28:03.920 --> 28:05.500
to build a mathematical rescue.

28:05.640 --> 28:06.280
A rescue.

28:06.480 --> 28:08.460
A major paper by rule, et al.

28:09.020 --> 28:14.040
In 2020, they looked at this drift and proposed that the chaos isn't totally random.

28:14.660 --> 28:17.320
They introduced the concept of a null coding space.

28:17.440 --> 28:18.220
Null coding space?

28:18.280 --> 28:19.660
This sounds like quantum physics.

28:20.120 --> 28:21.820
How does this rescue the banking app?

28:22.000 --> 28:26.680
Imagine the entire chaotic firing pattern of the thousands of neurons in the mouse's

28:26.680 --> 28:29.960
brain as a giant morphing three-dimensional cloud.

28:30.480 --> 28:30.500
Okay.

28:30.680 --> 28:31.420
A 3D cloud.

28:31.620 --> 28:36.000
Within that swirling cloud, there is one specific angle, one highly precise mathematical

28:36.000 --> 28:39.120
axis you can draw through it that carries the specific information.

28:39.280 --> 28:40.220
Turn left now.

28:40.640 --> 28:43.280
So that axis is the task-relevant dimension.

28:43.280 --> 28:43.880
Exactly.

28:43.880 --> 28:45.220
Rule et al.

28:45.220 --> 28:51.840
Argued that the neural cloud can twist, expand, drift, and swap neurons all at once, as long

28:51.840 --> 28:57.400
as it only moves in directions that are perpendicular or orthogonal to that specific turn left axis.

28:57.560 --> 28:57.980
Oh, I see.

28:58.080 --> 29:02.240
So as long as the cloud's movement doesn't interfere with that one specific beam of information,

29:02.520 --> 29:04.600
the rest of the brain doesn't care what the cloud is doing.

29:04.980 --> 29:08.020
Think of the 3D cloud casting a 2D shadow on a wall.

29:08.020 --> 29:12.920
The shadow is the specific information the motor cortex needs to actually move the mouse's legs?

29:13.140 --> 29:13.380
Yes.

29:13.780 --> 29:19.280
The 3D cloud, the raw physical geometry of the neurons, can completely change its shape,

29:19.760 --> 29:20.780
rotate, and deform.

29:21.420 --> 29:26.760
But as long as it deforms in a way that continues to cast the exact same 2D shadow on the wall,

29:27.180 --> 29:28.320
the mouse will turn left.

29:28.780 --> 29:33.980
So the readout, the decodable shadow, remains stable, even as the physical object casting the

29:33.980 --> 29:35.520
shadow is destroyed and rebuilt.

29:35.740 --> 29:37.260
It is a brilliant mathematical rescue.

29:37.260 --> 29:40.020
It explains how behavior survives physical drift.

29:40.340 --> 29:44.220
But Flyxion looks at this rescue and identifies a massive fatal flaw.

29:44.440 --> 29:44.780
They do.

29:45.220 --> 29:50.820
Flyxion says the rule at all, rescue subtly but fundamentally, changes the rules of reality.

29:51.120 --> 29:54.780
Well, we started this deep dive agreeing that the physical structure of the network,

29:54.900 --> 29:57.640
the manifold, the synapses, was the conserved object.

29:57.800 --> 29:58.760
That was the reality.

29:58.940 --> 29:59.220
Right.

29:59.620 --> 30:05.380
Now, to explain drift, rule at all, are saying the conserved object isn't physical at all.

30:05.380 --> 30:11.580
The conserved object is simply what a scientist can mathematically decode from a snapshot of

30:11.580 --> 30:13.600
the network at a specific millisecond.

30:14.300 --> 30:14.940
Decodability.

30:15.340 --> 30:15.620
Ah.

30:16.480 --> 30:22.580
And Flyxion says a 2D shadow, a static decodable snapshot, is not a robust enough architecture

30:22.580 --> 30:27.880
to explain how a biological organism actually survives in the chaotic real world.

30:27.880 --> 30:31.480
Which brings us to the absolute core insight of Flyxion's entire theory.

30:31.700 --> 30:36.640
If the physical manifold drifts away and simple decodability isn't strong enough, what

30:36.640 --> 30:39.580
is the true hidden architecture that preserves your mind?

30:39.700 --> 30:44.240
To reveal this architecture, Flyxion demands that we make a vital, non-negotiable distinction

30:44.240 --> 30:47.640
between two concepts, decodability and reachability.

30:48.020 --> 30:49.340
Decodability and reachability.

30:49.340 --> 30:49.700
Yes.

30:49.820 --> 30:52.480
If we confuse these two, we misunderstand the brain entirely.

30:52.580 --> 30:52.720
Okay.

30:52.760 --> 30:53.680
Let's define them deeply.

30:54.000 --> 30:54.860
What is decodability?

30:55.700 --> 30:58.340
Decodability is an obsession with the present moment.

30:59.060 --> 31:02.360
It is a static, frozen snapshot of time.

31:02.880 --> 31:04.280
It asks a single question.

31:04.380 --> 31:04.900
Which is?

31:05.120 --> 31:11.580
Given the electrical state of the brain right now, at this exact millisecond, can I extract

31:11.580 --> 31:12.560
the correct information?

31:13.280 --> 31:17.160
Can I look at the shadow on a wall and decode that the mouse intends to turn left?

31:17.160 --> 31:20.220
So decodability cares about what is.

31:20.360 --> 31:20.560
Yes.

31:21.160 --> 31:21.900
And reachability.

31:22.100 --> 31:23.720
Reachability is about potential energy.

31:23.920 --> 31:24.840
It is about the future.

31:25.100 --> 31:25.540
Exactly.

31:26.140 --> 31:31.560
It asks, from this current frozen state, what futures are still available to the system?

31:32.000 --> 31:33.300
What can the system still do?

31:33.980 --> 31:35.800
Reachability cares about what could be.

31:36.120 --> 31:36.320
Okay.

31:36.400 --> 31:38.660
Let me build an analogy here to make this crystal clear.

31:39.180 --> 31:43.420
Imagine you are standing on an overpass, looking down at two cars driving side by side

31:43.420 --> 31:44.380
on the highway below you.

31:44.460 --> 31:45.380
They are both heading north.

31:45.540 --> 31:45.780
Okay.

31:46.160 --> 31:46.580
Two cars.

31:46.580 --> 31:47.860
You have a police radar gun.

31:47.960 --> 31:48.640
That's your decoder.

31:48.860 --> 31:50.620
You point the radar gun and take a snapshot.

31:51.000 --> 31:54.580
The decoder says car A is going 60 miles per hour north.

31:55.100 --> 31:57.080
Car B is going 60 miles per hour north.

31:57.580 --> 31:59.340
Decodably, these two cars are identical.

31:59.520 --> 31:59.820
Yes.

32:00.100 --> 32:03.240
In this exact millisecond, they are doing the exact same thing.

32:03.520 --> 32:05.320
Or perfect static snapshot.

32:05.540 --> 32:05.700
Yeah.

32:05.700 --> 32:09.280
But the static snapshot is hiding a lethal reality.

32:09.980 --> 32:10.100
Right.

32:10.300 --> 32:13.640
Because car A has a completely full tank of gas.

32:13.640 --> 32:19.180
Car B, however, has a massive hole in the gas tank and is literally running on fumes.

32:20.020 --> 32:22.620
Car A has a massive set of places it can drive.

32:22.700 --> 32:24.020
It can keep going north to Canada.

32:24.300 --> 32:26.160
It can take the next exit and go to the mountains.

32:26.320 --> 32:27.320
It can turn around.

32:27.560 --> 32:28.100
And car B.

32:28.320 --> 32:33.080
Car B is going to stall and die on the side of the highway in exactly two miles.

32:33.340 --> 32:33.600
Wow.

32:33.600 --> 32:37.840
If you only look at the static snapshot, the decodability, they are the same.

32:38.280 --> 32:44.360
But their reachability, the invisible map of futures available to them, is fundamentally totally different.

32:44.400 --> 32:47.620
And this is Flyxion's staggering insight regarding the drifting brain.

32:48.400 --> 32:54.160
A neural population can perfectly preserve its decodable readout while its reachability is secretly collapsing.

32:54.300 --> 32:55.340
Just like the gas tank.

32:55.340 --> 32:55.900
Exactly.

32:56.460 --> 33:01.280
When the mouse's brain drifts over weeks, rule it all, looked at the data and said,

33:01.660 --> 33:04.140
it's fine, we can still decode the turn left signal.

33:04.420 --> 33:07.080
The shadow looks right, the cars are both going 60.

33:07.340 --> 33:12.660
But Flyxion says, wait, what happens if the mouse trips over its own feet mid-maze?

33:12.920 --> 33:14.920
What happens if a loud noise startles it?

33:14.980 --> 33:15.440
Recovery.

33:16.160 --> 33:19.060
The real world isn't a perfect frictionless vacuum.

33:19.280 --> 33:19.760
Exactly.

33:20.000 --> 33:23.740
If a distraction happens mid-task, decodability is useless.

33:23.740 --> 33:28.760
Decodability only tells you that the mouse was thinking turn left right before it tripped.

33:29.080 --> 33:32.780
It doesn't tell you if the brain has the capacity to recover from the error.

33:33.060 --> 33:39.340
Because to recover from a trip, the brain needs to instantly access a completely different trajectory to get back on track.

33:39.520 --> 33:41.380
It needs a full tank of gas.

33:41.500 --> 33:43.780
It needs a robust map of reachable futures.

33:44.100 --> 33:48.260
And Flyxion gives a formal mathematical name to this invisible map of futures.

33:48.600 --> 33:50.920
They call it the admissible continuation set.

33:50.920 --> 33:53.800
In the math, it is written as gamma C of X.

33:54.200 --> 33:55.840
Let's translate this math into English.

33:55.980 --> 33:57.840
What exactly is gamma C of X?

33:57.980 --> 34:01.420
Flyxion defines it with brutal precision in the appendices of the paper.

34:01.980 --> 34:06.120
The admissible continuation set is the complete set of all possible trajectories,

34:06.320 --> 34:10.020
all possible paths, starting from your current biological state, X.

34:10.120 --> 34:11.320
Okay, starting from X.

34:11.400 --> 34:14.260
That obey the system's physical transition laws, F.

34:14.380 --> 34:15.340
The laws of physics.

34:15.340 --> 34:21.740
Right, and continue to satisfy all the task constraints, C, until the job is successfully finished.

34:22.080 --> 34:28.960
So applying that to our highway analogy, the state, X, is where the car is located right now, going 60 miles per hour.

34:29.120 --> 34:29.300
Yeah.

34:29.560 --> 34:33.900
The transition laws, F, are the laws of physics and the mechanical limits of the car.

34:34.220 --> 34:35.180
It can't fly.

34:35.280 --> 34:36.960
The steering wheel can only turn so far.

34:37.100 --> 34:37.840
Friction exists.

34:37.980 --> 34:38.200
Perfect.

34:38.200 --> 34:41.540
And the constraints, C, are the rules of survival.

34:41.880 --> 34:43.720
You can't drive into oncoming traffic.

34:43.960 --> 34:48.080
You have to stay on the pavement, and you have to reach your destination before you run out of gas.

34:48.280 --> 34:48.880
Precisely.

34:49.520 --> 34:53.300
The admissible continuation set is not a physical object in the brain.

34:53.960 --> 35:03.140
It is the invisible mathematical map of every single valid successful path the neural network could possibly take from this exact millisecond forward.

35:03.140 --> 35:11.480
And Flyxion mathematically proves, in Appendix B, that you can have a perfect decodable signal while your continuation set is actively shrinking to zero.

35:11.880 --> 35:15.560
Let's walk through Appendix B, because this is where the theory gets real teeth.

35:15.700 --> 35:15.920
Yeah.

35:16.060 --> 35:19.440
How do they prove that decodability and reachability come completely apart?

35:19.820 --> 35:21.600
They set up a minimal worked example.

35:22.240 --> 35:26.020
Imagine a system defined by just two coordinates, A and B.

35:26.600 --> 35:28.440
Let's tie this to biology to make it real.

35:28.440 --> 35:28.880
Okay.

35:29.080 --> 35:34.380
Let's say A is the decodable firing rate of the neurons controlling the mouse's legs, the speed of the car.

35:34.580 --> 35:34.940
And B?

35:35.440 --> 35:42.900
Let's say B is a hidden, unread biological variable, like the buildup of calcium ions inside the cells, which causes fatigue.

35:43.380 --> 35:43.660
All right.

35:43.720 --> 35:46.040
So we have leg speed and calcium fatigue.

35:46.400 --> 35:55.660
The rules of this mathematical system are that A, the leg speed, stays perfectly constant, but B, the calcium fatigue, grows exponentially over time.

35:55.820 --> 35:57.120
And the constraint of the system.

35:57.120 --> 36:07.040
The constraint is that if B gets too large, if the calcium buildup hits a critical threshold, the cells undergo a toxic shock, and the system catastrophically fails.

36:07.480 --> 36:08.380
The mouse freezes.

36:08.500 --> 36:08.720
Okay.

36:08.820 --> 36:12.660
So we start the simulation with two different brain states, state X and state Y.

36:12.760 --> 36:13.020
Yes.

36:13.260 --> 36:16.140
Both state X and state Y have the exact same value for A.

36:16.660 --> 36:20.360
The decoder looks at both brains and says, yep, the legs are moving at perfect speed.

36:20.760 --> 36:22.300
The shadow on the wall is identical.

36:22.620 --> 36:24.700
But the hidden variable B is different.

36:24.700 --> 36:31.240
State X starts with a tiny, microscopic value for B. Almost no calcium buildup.

36:31.720 --> 36:37.580
But state Y starts with a massive value for B. It is already hovering right below the toxic threshold.

36:38.060 --> 36:40.100
So what happens when we hit play on the simulation?

36:40.100 --> 36:42.400
Because B grows exponentially.

36:43.140 --> 36:47.800
State X has a long, luxurious runway before it ever has to worry about the failure threshold.

36:48.360 --> 36:50.800
It has a massive admissible continuation set.

36:51.100 --> 36:55.160
It has millions of valid futures where it successfully finishes the maze.

36:55.440 --> 36:56.380
But state Y?

36:56.660 --> 36:59.240
Because it started so dangerously close to the edge.

36:59.780 --> 37:02.700
Its B value explodes past the toxic limit almost instantly.

37:02.840 --> 37:03.340
It crashes.

37:03.560 --> 37:05.020
It has an empty continuation set.

37:05.280 --> 37:06.300
Zero valid futures.

37:06.300 --> 37:06.780
Wow.

37:07.680 --> 37:12.220
So to the scientists looking only at the decoder, state X and state Y look perfectly identical.

37:12.660 --> 37:14.560
They would publish a paper saying the brain was stable.

37:14.820 --> 37:15.120
Exactly.

37:15.260 --> 37:20.760
But to the brutal reality of the maze, state X was a guaranteed success and state Y was a dead mouse walking.

37:21.480 --> 37:26.060
Preserving the decodable signal is not the same thing as preserving the organism's ability to survive.

37:26.480 --> 37:28.320
This is the paradigm shift.

37:28.320 --> 37:37.600
But Flyxion notes that this concept is somewhat structurally similar to Aubin's viability kernel from control theory, but with a massive philosophical difference.

37:37.700 --> 37:38.480
What's the difference?

37:38.780 --> 37:44.820
Viability theory usually assumes an external controller, a driver actively steering the car to keep it inside the safe zone.

37:45.440 --> 37:50.000
But Flyxion's continuation set is applied to uncontrolled population trajectories.

37:50.460 --> 37:52.360
Uncontrolled meaning no director.

37:52.360 --> 38:02.180
It maps what the biological network can naturally reach on its own through its own internal momentum without a homunculus or a brainwave acting as a driver.

38:02.480 --> 38:07.420
We have just built an incredibly beautiful, entirely invisible theoretical architecture.

38:07.640 --> 38:11.620
I mean, the true ghost in the machine is not a wave and it's not a static physical wire.

38:11.900 --> 38:14.800
It is the mathematical map of reachable futures.

38:15.160 --> 38:16.060
It's breathtaking.

38:16.060 --> 38:23.060
But as fascinating as that is, we can't just invent a cool math concept, drop the mic, and say neuroscience is solved.

38:23.460 --> 38:25.720
A theory is only as good as its testability.

38:26.080 --> 38:27.080
Flyxion knows this.

38:27.640 --> 38:28.940
They don't just offer philosophy.

38:29.160 --> 38:36.040
They offer a rigorous blueprint for exactly how to prove themselves right and, more importantly, how to prove themselves wrong.

38:36.240 --> 38:38.780
This is the mark of truly elite science.

38:38.920 --> 38:39.360
It is.

38:39.660 --> 38:45.000
They dedicate Section 7 of their paper to defining the discriminating tests.

38:45.000 --> 38:50.620
How do we actually test if an invisible mathematical map is the true boss of the brain?

38:51.200 --> 38:55.200
Flyxion gives us two paths, a practical one and a holy grail one.

38:55.500 --> 38:58.320
Let's start with the practical one, the reanalysis test.

38:58.400 --> 39:01.420
So this doesn't require new, expensive animal surgeries.

39:01.580 --> 39:01.760
No.

39:02.040 --> 39:05.620
The brilliance of the reanalysis test is that the data is already sitting on servers.

39:05.620 --> 39:16.780
We just need to go back to the raw data from Sadler and Golub's monkey BCI experiments and Driscoll's drifting mouse experiments and look at it through the lens of reachability instead of decodability.

39:17.040 --> 39:18.940
What exactly are we looking for in that old data?

39:19.380 --> 39:21.560
We are hunting for the edge cases, the failures.

39:22.060 --> 39:23.020
Let's take the drifting mice.

39:23.680 --> 39:28.880
The decodability camp says, as long as the shadow on the wall is stable, the mouse succeeds.

39:28.880 --> 39:29.940
And Flyxion says.

39:30.160 --> 39:36.080
Let's isolate the specific trials where the mouse stumbled or hesitated or got distracted.

39:36.280 --> 39:39.000
The moments where they needed a full tank of gas to recover?

39:39.200 --> 39:39.600
Exactly.

39:40.200 --> 39:49.700
Flyxion predicts that if you calculate the admissible continuation set for the mouse's brain state in the milliseconds right before it stumbled, you will find the true predictor of success.

39:50.020 --> 39:50.600
How so?

39:50.600 --> 40:00.620
If the mathematical continuation set was large, if the mouse had a robust map of valid recovery paths, the mouse recovered from the stumble and finished the maze.

40:00.780 --> 40:01.940
Okay, but if it was small?

40:02.120 --> 40:13.260
If the physical drift had warped the network such that the continuation set was narrow and brittle, even if the static decodable shadow looked perfectly fine, the mouse failed to recover and lost the trial.

40:13.260 --> 40:15.960
That is such a clean, devastating test.

40:16.380 --> 40:21.980
You find the trials where the decoder says the mouse is perfectly fine, but the reachability mask says the mouse is completely doomed.

40:22.200 --> 40:22.480
Yes.

40:22.660 --> 40:27.620
If the mouse actually fails in reality on those specific trials, Flyxion is vindicated.

40:28.140 --> 40:32.760
The invisible map of futures is a better predictor of reality than the static snapshot.

40:33.340 --> 40:36.500
And you apply the exact same logic to the BCI monkeys.

40:36.500 --> 40:45.180
Find a trial where the perturbed mapping was technically inside the monkey's physical manifold, so traditional theory predicts they should learn it easily.

40:45.680 --> 40:49.620
But the required continuation path was mathematically inaccessible.

40:49.740 --> 40:50.520
And if they fail?

40:50.720 --> 40:59.100
If the monkey's learning success tracks closer with the size of their continuation set than with simple manifold membership, the paradigm officially shifts.

40:59.460 --> 41:00.740
That's the practical test.

41:01.140 --> 41:02.800
But what is the new experiment?

41:03.340 --> 41:04.120
The Holy Grail.

41:04.120 --> 41:09.500
Because this is where Flyxion invites the wave theorists back into the room for the ultimate showdown.

41:10.020 --> 41:12.880
The Holy Grail experiment is a monumental technical challenge.

41:13.360 --> 41:20.240
But theoretically, it is the only way to definitively settle the debate between spatial computing and continuation dynamics.

41:20.420 --> 41:21.160
What do we have to do?

41:21.380 --> 41:24.220
We need to observe an animal performing a complex task.

41:24.220 --> 41:34.480
And we need to independently manipulate the brain waves, the oscillatory phase, while holding the underlying physical geometry of the neuronal firing perfectly unnaturally fixed.

41:34.580 --> 41:36.400
Wait, how is that even physically possible?

41:36.540 --> 41:38.200
How do you separate the wave from the water?

41:38.540 --> 41:41.660
It requires advanced, highly targeted optogenetics.

41:42.200 --> 41:46.640
You use lasers to stimulate specific neurons modified with light-sensitive proteins.

41:46.840 --> 41:47.840
Lasers in the brain.

41:47.840 --> 41:48.080
Yeah.

41:48.640 --> 41:59.180
You would have to pulse the lasers in a way that shifts the timing and phase of the macroscopic brain waves without altering the base firing rates or the physical covariance of the underlying network.

41:59.300 --> 42:00.280
Has anyone done this?

42:00.660 --> 42:09.640
Flyxion cites a 2026 paper by Chen et al., which managed to induce phase mismatch, resulting in the animals making behavioral errors.

42:09.640 --> 42:15.900
Ah. And Miller's camp immediately pointed to this and said, see, we messed with the wave and the animal failed.

42:16.320 --> 42:17.620
The wave is the boss.

42:18.200 --> 42:22.100
They did. But Flyxion says we have to look much deeper at why the animal failed.

42:22.660 --> 42:28.800
When we manipulate the wave with lasers, there are two possible outcomes, and they define two entirely different versions of reality.

42:29.040 --> 42:30.880
Walk us through outcome number one.

42:31.280 --> 42:38.120
Outcome number one. We hit the brain with the laser, we shift the wave phase, and the animal changes its behavior or makes an error.

42:38.120 --> 42:50.900
But when we crunch the math on the neural data, we see that the manipulated wave simply pushed the brain down a different path that already existed inside the pre-existing admissible continuation set.

42:51.100 --> 42:58.640
Ah. Okay. The map of possible futures didn't change size or shape. The wave just chose a different road on the map.

42:58.640 --> 43:04.760
Exactly. If phase manipulation just changes which valid path is taken, then waves are merely selectors.

43:05.140 --> 43:09.700
They are a biological mechanism for picking an option, like a stage manager pointing left instead of right.

43:10.020 --> 43:18.140
But the stage itself, the hard mathematical reality of what paths actually exist, is dictated entirely by the continuation dynamics.

43:18.360 --> 43:20.520
Yes. In this outcome, Flyxion wins.

43:20.520 --> 43:32.540
I really need you, the listener, to grasp the philosophical difference here. There is a massive gulf between a force choosing a path that was already mathematically possible and a force creating the reality of the path itself.

43:32.760 --> 43:34.100
That is the crucial distinction.

43:34.360 --> 43:45.480
Like, if I am standing at a crossroads and a massive gust of wind blows me down the left path instead of the right path, the wind absolutely influenced my behavior. But the wind didn't pave the road.

43:45.480 --> 43:55.080
Right. Now let's look at outcome number two. We manipulate the wave phase with the laser, and the admissible continuation set itself alters, expands, or collapses.

43:55.380 --> 43:55.600
Oh.

43:56.020 --> 44:07.360
Holding all the physical wiring fixed, simply shifting the wave literally destroys or creates the map of reachable futures. Paths that were mathematically impossible a millisecond ago suddenly become valid.

44:07.520 --> 44:10.740
So the wind actually paves a new road and erases the old one.

44:10.740 --> 44:20.980
If that happens, then waves carry irreducible, causal, fundamental information. They aren't just selecting from a menu. They are cooking the food. They define the very physics of what is reachable.

44:21.080 --> 44:22.600
And if outcome two happens...

44:22.600 --> 44:25.260
If outcome two happens, spatial computing wins the war.

44:25.340 --> 44:25.660
Ah.

44:25.900 --> 44:33.240
Miller, Brinkat, and Roy are completely vindicated, and Flyxion admits that their continuation math missed a causally relevant variable.

44:33.240 --> 44:42.840
This is what I respect so much about this paper. Flyxion is defining the exact mathematically rigid conditions under which their own life's work would be proven wrong.

44:43.440 --> 44:47.440
They are building the arena and handing a loaded weapon to their opponent.

44:47.980 --> 44:56.580
It is the ultimate display of scientific confidence. It forces the field to stop relying on loose correlations and start proving causation.

44:56.580 --> 45:09.700
So we've journeyed through the mechanical nightmare of routing information, the digital proof of waveless RNNs, the terrifying shifting sands of representational drift, and the invisible mathematical maps of reachable futures.

45:09.820 --> 45:10.080
Of what?

45:10.480 --> 45:21.240
It is. But there's an elephant in the room. A massive philosophical elephant. We have to apply this rigorous continuation lens to the biggest mystery in all of human inquiry.

45:21.360 --> 45:22.180
Consciousness itself.

45:22.180 --> 45:31.840
Exactly. Because Miller's spatial computing theory doesn't limit itself to simply explaining how a mouse turns left in a maze. They make the grand leap to explaining consciousness.

45:32.240 --> 45:36.460
And they lean very heavily on the phenomenon of anesthesia to prove their point.

45:36.580 --> 45:41.240
The anesthesia argument. This is section 8 of the source paper. What exactly is Miller claiming here?

45:41.520 --> 45:47.840
Miller argues that the loss of consciousness under anesthesia is perfectly correlated with the desynchronization of brain waves.

45:47.840 --> 45:55.400
When you are awake, alert, and conscious, your cortical waves are fast, mixed, complex, and highly organized.

45:55.520 --> 45:55.800
Right.

45:55.980 --> 46:04.920
When the anesthesiologist pushes propofol into your IV, your brain waves don't stop, but they collapse into this desynchronized, high-power, slow delta activity.

46:05.080 --> 46:12.400
The waves get huge, slow, and dumb. The spotlight becomes a floodlight that just blindly washes over the whole stage.

46:12.400 --> 46:20.740
Precisely. They cite a fascinating 2023 paper by Cabral et al., who used ultra-fast fMRI to watch this happen in rats.

46:20.900 --> 46:21.960
What did Cabral find?

46:22.520 --> 46:30.840
Cabral showed that the awake, conscious rat brain maintains these spontaneous, highly organized standing wave modes that echo across distant regions of the cortex.

46:31.620 --> 46:38.560
But as you increase the dose of the anesthetic, the resonance quality of these waves, what physicists call the Q-factor, declines systematically.

46:38.560 --> 46:40.040
The Q-factor drops.

46:40.240 --> 46:45.840
Yes. Under deep anesthesia, the beautiful organization collapses into near a periodic fluctuation.

46:46.220 --> 46:48.720
The organization is gone, and the consciousness is gone.

46:48.940 --> 46:54.720
So Miller looks at this and says, look, the waves lose their complex shape, and the mind disappears.

46:55.400 --> 46:58.940
Therefore, the complex waves are the mind. The waves are doing the thinking.

46:59.520 --> 47:01.640
And here's where Flyxion brings down the hammer.

47:02.640 --> 47:06.920
Flyxion argues that Miller is fundamentally confusing two very different jobs.

47:07.040 --> 47:07.660
Do jobs.

47:07.660 --> 47:08.060
Yes.

47:09.040 --> 47:17.220
Flyxion agrees that the fMRI data supports waves doing job one, but argues it absolutely does not support them doing job two.

47:17.640 --> 47:20.480
Let's clearly separate the two jobs. What is job one?

47:21.160 --> 47:24.380
Job one is realizing the fixed physical geometry of the brain.

47:24.960 --> 47:28.760
It's establishing the overall anatomical envelope of what is reachable at all.

47:28.880 --> 47:29.440
The envelope.

47:29.640 --> 47:34.100
Yes. Cabral's rat data strongly suggests that these standing waves are eigenmodes.

47:34.100 --> 47:42.240
They are the natural resonant frequencies of the brain's physical shape, much like how a guitar string vibrates at a specific frequency based on its length and tension.

47:42.660 --> 47:46.400
They are the background hum of the biological hardware being turned on and energized.

47:46.400 --> 47:49.740
They set the baseline envelope of gamma C of X.

47:49.960 --> 47:57.420
So the waves are the city's power grid. When you turn on the power grid, the traffic lights work, the computers turn on, and the city can function.

47:57.420 --> 47:57.940
Exactly.

47:57.940 --> 48:07.480
When you anesthetize the rat, you are browning out the power grid. The voltage drops, the traffic lights flash randomly, and the city shuts down. That's job one.

48:07.480 --> 48:25.880
Correct. But spatial computing requires waves to do job two. Job two is the precise, moment-to-moment selection and routing of specific thoughts. Job two is deciding that this specific pattern of light hitting the retina means turn left on this specific trial.

48:26.380 --> 48:34.920
And Flyxion is saying that just because browning out the power grid stops the city from functioning, that doesn't mean the electricity itself is the mayor making the complex zoning decisions.

48:34.920 --> 48:47.440
Exactly. Equating the power grid with the mayor is a massive category error. Proving that complex waves are required to energize the boundary of what is reachable does not prove that waves determine what gets selected inside that boundary.

48:47.680 --> 48:48.700
That makes total sense.

48:49.000 --> 48:53.900
Miller takes the undeniable evidence for job one and smuggles it in as proof for job two.

48:54.380 --> 49:01.240
Flyxion doesn't just stop at Miller, do they? They take aim at the entire landscape of modern consciousness theories.

49:01.240 --> 49:07.400
They do. They draw direct parallels to integrated information theory, or IIT, and global workspace theory.

49:08.000 --> 49:13.580
All of these major celebrated theories suffer from the exact same logical leap.

49:13.920 --> 49:14.740
They find a correlate.

49:14.980 --> 49:25.920
Right. They find a property that is reliably associated with consciousness, whether it's wave organization or a global broadcast mechanism or a complex mathematical metric of integrated information.

49:26.100 --> 49:28.260
They find the biological or mathematical correlate.

49:28.260 --> 49:36.040
And then they mistakenly treat the mere presence of that correlate as the explanation for how subjective experience is actually generated.

49:36.200 --> 49:40.000
They find footprints in the mud and declare the footprints are the animal.

49:40.360 --> 49:48.040
Flyxion demands that the field of consciousness research apply the exact same rigorous discipline used on the mixed selectivity routing problem.

49:48.040 --> 49:52.000
We must distinguish what is correlated from what is causally required.

49:52.000 --> 50:06.360
The fact that wave organization correlates with conscious states, even with perfect dose-dependent precision under anesthesia, is not an explanation of why organization of that particular kind generates the feeling of being alive.

50:06.520 --> 50:07.600
Okay, I have to stop here.

50:07.720 --> 50:11.620
I have to ask the question that I know you, the listener, are screaming at your phone right now.

50:11.720 --> 50:12.120
What is it?

50:12.120 --> 50:15.060
Let's assume Flyxion is completely right.

50:15.460 --> 50:17.840
The manifold structure is just the physical limit.

50:18.540 --> 50:23.140
The admissible continuations are the invisible mathematical map of all possible futures.

50:23.500 --> 50:27.400
And the brain waves are just the biological power grid that lights up the map.

50:28.040 --> 50:30.640
If all of that is true, what is actually making the choice?

50:30.760 --> 50:33.560
What pushes us down one continuation path instead of another?

50:33.900 --> 50:35.460
Who is driving the car?

50:35.460 --> 50:40.160
That is the beautiful, terrifying void at the center of Flyxion's paper.

50:40.940 --> 50:44.380
Flyxion does not claim to have the answer to what the ultimate selector is.

50:44.420 --> 50:45.000
They don't know.

50:45.200 --> 50:52.920
They concede that oscillatory processes, waves, might be one of the biological mechanisms that participates in selecting among the futures.

50:53.220 --> 50:59.240
But they are brutally honest that their mathematical model leaves the ultimate driver undefined.

50:59.240 --> 51:06.040
Flyxion defines the exact physics of the maze and the map of every possible path through it.

51:06.380 --> 51:10.560
But they do not pretend to know what gives the mouse its soul.

51:10.760 --> 51:11.380
It's haunting.

51:12.100 --> 51:16.700
We've replaced a false mechanical certainty with a mathematically precise mystery.

51:16.840 --> 51:17.560
Yes, we have.

51:17.720 --> 51:19.600
Which brings us to the end of our deep dive.

51:20.020 --> 51:22.700
Let's summarize the massive journey we have just taken.

51:23.060 --> 51:27.540
We started by looking under the hood of the brain, expecting to find the gears of cognition.

51:27.540 --> 51:30.740
And we found a war between waves and math.

51:30.980 --> 51:35.180
We can summarize the new paradigm, the layered view that Flyxion proposes.

51:35.400 --> 51:37.840
It isn't about entirely destroying the wave theory.

51:38.000 --> 51:43.740
It's about stripping it of its unearned causal power and putting it in its proper biological place.

51:43.860 --> 51:44.760
Okay, what are the layers?

51:44.940 --> 51:50.240
Layer one is the manifold structure, the physical wiring, the 86 billion neurons, the synapses.

51:50.540 --> 51:53.900
This defines the absolute rigid physical limits of the system.

51:54.000 --> 51:56.100
You cannot play notes that do not exist on the piano.

51:56.100 --> 51:56.700
Got it.

51:56.820 --> 51:57.420
And layer two.

51:57.540 --> 52:04.620
Layer two is the admissible continuations, the gamma C of X, the hidden, invisible, dynamic map

52:04.620 --> 52:11.880
that plots out all the possible, reachable, successful futures from whatever state you're currently in.

52:12.000 --> 52:15.300
This is the true underlying conserved object.

52:15.560 --> 52:15.920
Yes.

52:16.520 --> 52:22.560
This is the mathematical architecture that allows a mouse or a human to survive and maintain their behavior,

52:22.560 --> 52:26.500
even when their physical wiring is constantly drifting and scrambling itself.

52:26.500 --> 52:28.940
And layer three is the biological mechanism.

52:29.000 --> 52:31.080
This is where the brain waves and oscillations live.

52:31.260 --> 52:31.420
Yeah.

52:31.660 --> 52:39.760
These mechanisms might be the specific biological tools the brain evolved to highlight and select among those mathematically possible futures in real time.

52:39.920 --> 52:42.080
They are the power grid and perhaps the selector.

52:42.300 --> 52:42.540
Right.

52:42.540 --> 52:45.460
But they do not define the space of what is reachable.

52:45.460 --> 52:46.420
They traverse the map.

52:46.420 --> 52:47.300
They do not draw it.

52:47.300 --> 52:49.820
I want to bring this all the way down to you, the listener.

52:49.820 --> 52:56.880
Throughout this hour, we've talked about macaque monkeys, moving cursors, digital RNNs solving dot problems on servers,

52:56.880 --> 52:59.220
and mice running on virtual treadmills.

52:59.220 --> 53:02.400
But this entire conversation has been about the engine inside your head.

53:02.880 --> 53:09.860
Right now, as you process the final words of this sentence, the physical manifold of your brain is subtly drifting.

53:10.500 --> 53:18.320
The exact synaptic connections that are firing right now to understand my voice might be assigned to a completely different task a month from now.

53:18.320 --> 53:26.280
The physical hardware that stores your memories, your skills, and your personality is a shifting, unstable river of biology.

53:26.860 --> 53:33.660
And yet, despite that total physical instability, you woke up every single morning feeling like a unified, continuous person.

53:33.960 --> 53:35.120
You remember your name.

53:35.480 --> 53:36.700
You know how to drive your car.

53:36.820 --> 53:38.700
Your sense of humor remains intact.

53:38.840 --> 53:45.420
Your behavior and your subjective experience remain flawlessly stable across a completely unstable physical substrate.

53:45.600 --> 53:48.140
So I want to leave you with a final provocative thought.

53:48.320 --> 53:55.820
Something that wasn't explicitly debated in Flyxion's paper, but is the inevitable, staggering consequence of everything they have built here.

53:55.940 --> 53:56.800
That's a big question.

53:56.800 --> 54:06.700
If your brain's physical wiring is constantly drifting, and a simple static snapshot of your brain isn't enough to explain how you recover from mistakes and navigate the chaos of the world,

54:07.100 --> 54:11.280
and the electrical waves are just the power grid, then what actually preserves you?

54:11.340 --> 54:13.660
Are we just a mathematical pattern of reachable futures?

54:13.660 --> 54:21.320
If the admissible continuation set is the only thing that actually stays consistent, or your biology morphs and dies and rebuilds around it,

54:21.580 --> 54:23.960
it implies something profound about human identity.

54:24.360 --> 54:31.020
It implies that we are not defined by what we are physically doing right now in this exact fleeting millisecond snapshot.

54:31.020 --> 54:35.640
We are defined strictly by the invisible map of what we are capable of doing next.

54:36.120 --> 54:37.460
You are your continuations.

54:38.000 --> 54:43.360
The engine under the hood of your consciousness isn't made of metal or waves or even biology.

54:43.700 --> 54:46.000
It is made of pure mathematical potential.

